body:
@isuruf I agree it would be nice to fix this. What happens currently is on each iteration of training/inference we reclaim the memory from the previous run. After warmup and recording, that memory is marked so that it if it is accessed it errors.

Fast-path execution caches the output tensor objects, so as is we can't directly set the tensors to [error on access](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/torch/_inductor/cudagraph_trees.py#L2127) because they are the same tensors we return in the next invocation. But, we could potentially exclude these tensors from output caching, similar to what we do with [aliased tensors]( https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/0a038cf0cff2d071b7359ac0491fd2ba7798a438/torch/_inductor/cudagraph_trees.py#L1231). 

My concern is this potentially a pessimization. For training, there are thousands of output tensors. The prior cudagraphs backend which this is replacing did not have any error checking for overwriting live memory so checking on first and second iteration (and then again on any recompilation) was an improvement. In any case it's worth implementing, seeing if there is a regression, and going from there.
